  <h2>The stages of a lighting design</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>The intent</strong>: atmospheres, creative approach, key moments of the running order.</li>
    <li><strong>The lighting plot</strong>: positions and choice of fixtures (Vectorworks Spotlight).</li>
    <li><strong>Previsualisation</strong>: 3D rendering of the looks to approve before ordering.</li>
    <li><strong>The specifications</strong>: equipment list and requirements for consulting the lighting supplier.</li>
    <li><strong>On site</strong>: follow-up of programming and focusing through to the show.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>For which events?</h2>
  <p>Concerts, fashion shows and luxury events, ceremonies, conventions, heritage venues, scenography and festivals. The design can also start from an existing one to be adapted to a new venue, to the available power or to a budget.</p>

  <h2>Constraints to factor in from the start</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The venue's rigging points and permissible loads.</li>
    <li>The available power and its distribution.</li>
    <li>The time for load-in and focusing.</li>
    <li>The safety constraints of the venue and the audience.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What ESE does</h2>
  <p>ESE designs event lighting with Beat Per Light, its regular lighting design partner, and makes it deliverable through its experience in technical direction and production management. References: A$AP Rocky at the Accor Arena, BSK at the Trianon and the Élysée Montmartre.</p>
